## R22-01: a rooted grant chain needs attenuation and an actual authority root

Sections 4.1/7.1 conflate authority with competence. Authority to attest a probe does not establish ability to run it faithfully; this distinction is in the cited ontology itself. A power is an authorization/capability grant, not an empirical competence record or a duty.

Countermodel: A has release power over duty D, and signs a child grant giving B global migrate_profile or open_for rights. The chain roots in a legitimate party, meeting rule 1 as written, but B's rights were never A's to delegate. Also a trust snapshot can bind a key to A without authorizing A to create obligations for C. Rooting at a recognized key must not make every act authoritative.

Purpose is a persistent generative orientation, while a power is permission for particular acts. Adopting a purpose can motivate or authorize specified self-directed work; it does not by itself confer authority over others. A standing grant may implement one operational consequence of a purpose without being its definition.

Require explicit root grants accepted by the recipient; each edge must be signed by a grantor with that act and delegation authority, and attenuate acts, subjects, beneficiaries, time and other limits. Evaluate expiry and conflicting grants at an explicit position/view. A holder cannot acquire duties through open_for unless its existing delegation or the governing assignment policy authorized that assignment. Define intake as nonbinding. Quotas are view-local unless a reservation/order/completeness mechanism is specified: two concurrent views with one slot left cannot promise a global one-slot bound merely by checking their own snapshots. Incoming consent/reassign mandates must retain WL02's distinction between releasing A and binding C.

## R22-02: falsifier coverage is not completeness or future reliability

Section 4.2 usefully separates fired from unevaluable. But no declared falsifier does not imply nothing can invalidate a closure. RELIABLE_UNFALSIFIABLE overstates an empty list.

Countermodel: A attests a closure with no triggers; later a valid counterexample shows its predicate never held. The list was empty, not logically exhaustive. Another case: a revocation/reopening is withheld from an incomplete snapshot. All declared falsifiers readable in that snapshot can be false while the recipient lacks relevant history. WRT-021 correctly reports completeness UNATTESTED; WRT-022 must not erase that distinction.

Use NO_DECLARED_FALSIFIERS (or equivalent neutral coverage status). Define reliance as eligibility for a named use under a recipient's policy, including validated closure, scope/binding, freshness, evidence basis and relevant view completeness assumptions. No-trigger-fired is one input, not proof of correctness or absence of unknown defeaters. Keep historical validity, present eligibility and coverage/freshness separate.

Record supersession and ordinary key retirement do not by themselves invalidate a historical signature or result. Such a trigger may intentionally require revalidation for future use, but must name that effect and its policy. Do not retroactively erase historical validity.

## R22-03: announced falsifiers cannot monopolize challenges; new children need authority

Section 4.2(3) permits NEW_EVIDENCE only if it fires an announced falsifier or changes the answer; section 4.3 instead cites SPEC section 7's broader syntactic test. Resolve this distinction explicitly.

Countermodel A: the closure author omitted atomicity as an assumption/trigger. A new witness demonstrates mixed snapshots on unchanged inputs, while the recorded canonical answer is unchanged. It is relevant new evidence against the closure/binding, but fires none of its author's announced triggers. Empty triggers must not make an error unchallengeable. Keep cheap automatic invalidation by declared triggers separate from a policy-governed route for new counterexamples and questions. Standing controls binding dispute effects; it does not make an outsider's independently checked counterexample false.

Countermodel B: X has no contest standing but can open its OWN duty serving parent P. P's child_opened_after trigger now makes its reliance STALE. This bypasses the contest-standing safeguard. A serves edge is declared intent, not permission to alter P's closure condition. Only an admitted child/enrolment or authorized amendment under P's policy should affect that condition. Require causal/admission order rather than relying solely on self-asserted timestamps for opened_after.

Countermodel C: the accepted slot values are A, B, then A. Under a time-varying observation, return to A can be a real fresh event affecting present obligations. Deduplicate identical content/semantic consequences without erasing occurrence, observation interval and provenance. Do not let new timestamps manufacture new semantic results either: specify the distinct novelty relations.

## R22-04: tier policy cannot replace the kinds of evidence with a universal ladder

Section 4.4 universally refuses irreversible closure on ATTESTED alone. That blocks legitimate performative acts: an authorized creditor's signed release or the owner's adoption can themselves be the act, rather than a weak report about a physical fact that a replay could supersede. It repeats the evidence/authority conflation in another direction.

Choose required evidence by act, consequence and trust model, with tiers supplying recipient-controlled defaults. The producer must not evade requirements by labeling an irreversible act reversible. Keep structured actor attestation distinguishable from observed execution; runner power authorizes the attestor and never proves that a run occurred.

The paper explicitly says evidence bases form a partial order. A universal weakest input basis may have no unique member for heterogeneous incomparable inputs. Retain per-input bases/domains/provenance and assumptions; a policy can derive a conservative summary when a meet is defined. Do not flatten observed, normative and formal inputs into one scalar or string minimum.

A signed profile migration gives provenance, not preservation of the old safety properties. Specify who may weaken which requirements, which meta-constraints survive, and how consumers opt into the successor lineage. Preserve the ability to leave a bad/lost lineage through an explicit new root rather than calling a locally valid new profile a continuation. No covert downgrade, but also no unchangeable profile sovereign over its operator.

## R22-05: a norm being in force differs from known compliance

Section 4.2(4) says a constraint holds until a counterexample. For a prohibition on disclosure, no leak record is not evidence no leak occurred; after a leak, the prohibition also need not cease to bind. Represent at least binding state separately from observed compliance/known violation and the relevant interval/coverage. A policy may remain ACTIVE with a known VIOLATION. No observation may be unknown without making the norm lapse. This preserves the useful constraint/invariant distinction without turning lack of a record into a world fact.

## R22-06: joint checkpoints must bind a pre-action shared view without self-reference

Section 4.8 is valuable but needs a precise object boundary. If store_snapshot includes the checkpoint itself or its changing co-signature envelope, constructing or co-signing it changes what it must hash. If it means the full local store, an unrelated record at one party makes an otherwise identical joint decision diverge, contradicting the stated shared-slice goal.

Define an addressed pre-action input manifest excluding the checkpoint and resulting act, the exact target action/terms, required signers and the shared relevant subgraph/trust/profile/evaluator commitments. Keep local full-view identities separately. A co-signature binds these exact bytes, not global completeness or unique consensus. Conflicting checkpoints/forks still require an ordering/conflict rule; signatures alone do not produce one shared irreversible past. Avoid counting a signer before all its actual acceptance requirements are met.

## R22-07: imported claim types need orthogonal roles and lifecycle

Candidate/measured/proved describe evidence status; stipulated/normative describe semantic force; debt is an unresolved edge, and withdrawn is lifecycle. A measured claim may later be withdrawn and must still retain what was measured. An adopted rule remains normative while its status changes. Prefer separate axes with explicit transitions over one enum that deletes these distinctions. The listed set is not a literal one-to-one transfer of the ontology's six source classes; this is an adaptation and needs its own loss report.

Likewise established (new postconditions) is not identical to preserved (what survives transformation), and not_established is not identical to a property actually lost. Do not mark those mappings gap-free without their domains being stated.

## Answers to the five review questions

1. Keep powers as typed grants, not special duties. Duties express owed performance; authority expresses admissible acts; competence needs its own observed evidence. Require explicit attenuation/root policies as above.
2. Start with a small closed/versioned vocabulary, but retain an explicit unknown/unassessed path and an admissible new-counterexample route. First review needs closure-condition/binding challenges beyond declared file hashes, and an authorized relevant-dependency enrolment rather than every serves edge.
3. Migration records make downgrades visible, not impossible. Recipient enforcement of who may weaken what, successor acceptance and surviving constraints is the missing part.
4. Re-verification is the sound default for a recipient claiming its own replay. Reuse verified local caches by exact computation/environment/verifier identity; any other shortcut must identify an accepted attestation or verifiable certificate and its own cost. No global cross-peer amortization is established by a signature or CAS address. A real CLI/runner contract remains a separate supported basis.
5. Use several axes, not six all-purpose mutually exclusive claim types. The minimal evidence depends on the target judgment; no signed label manufactures a measurement or proof.

## Smallest useful next step

P0 can record the CURRENT real review with correctly scoped delegated identities, enrolling public keys under an explicit recipient policy. Keys authenticate records; one operator still means one custody. Do not backdate real signatures onto historical reviews or require mythical agent-exclusive hardware custody before experimentation. Existing policy documents are an honest prose root until a real authority act is recorded.

P1 can implement path/manifest-scoped subjects and tri-state evaluation of a few revalidation triggers, including all operands (subject selector/current manifest/current profile) in the assessment identity. Choose dependency slices including relevant compiler/toolchain/config inputs, not merely paths that changed in the last patch. Keep empty trigger sets neutral and preserve the current reliance checks. Add vectors for unrelated change, relevant change, missing current revision and withheld/unknown dependency, without claiming global completeness.

Then implement crossing/replay binding and the minimal powers needed for the next consumer; contest and assignment cannot claim to enforce powers before those powers have a validator. P2 currently depends on P3 and the late-child invalidation rule depends on admission authority. Bring dependency contracts forward, rather than presenting the full P0–P8 sequence as already ordered by dependency. Neither all seven amendments nor all eight stages need become one giant implementation PR: fix the draft contracts locally and ship one coherent tested slice with its proposal.

No new owner questionnaire is needed for these engineering choices. Owner-controlled authority is needed only when the implementation actually asks a person or party to grant a new right, not to select internal data structures.

